(553601, 55133, 14253, '<td valign="top">Thanks for all the comments. I got this idea late in the challenge after my first idea didn''t pan out. Wasn''t really satisfied with final result but submitted to see how it was received.<br/> <br/>Metered with the sunglasses completely covering the lens, locked the exposure and moved the glasses away and shot. crop and autolevels.</td>', 'Thanks for all the comments. I got this idea late in the challenge after my first idea didn''t pan out. Wasn''t really satisfied with final result but submitted to see how it was received. Metered with the sunglasses completely covering the lens, locked the exposure and moved the glasses away and shot. crop and autolevels.', '2004-01-22 14:07:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(553627, 54513, 12962, '<td valign="top">Thanks all for the comments.  This photograph was taken from the 94th floor observation deck of the John Hancock Tower in Chicago - the same location from which achiral''s blue ribbon winning <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=41218" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=41218</a> (Sweet Home Chicago) was taken.  Archiral''s Sweet Home Chicago shot inspired me to also go around Chicago and take night shots including shots from the same location where he took his from  ... Chicago is such a photographer''s heaven and the 94th floor observation deck of the John Hancock Tower is a wonderful vantage point to shoot from ... and apparently can produce winning shots :-).  Again, thanks all.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2004-01-22 14:25:41.</i></td>', 'Thanks all for the comments.  This photograph was taken from the 94th floor observation deck of the John Hancock Tower in Chicago - the same location from which achiral''s blue ribbon winning //www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=41218 (Sweet Home Chicago) was taken.  Archiral''s Sweet Home Chicago shot inspired me to also go around Chicago and take night shots including shots from the same location where he took his from  ... Chicago is such a photographer''s heaven and the 94th floor observation deck of the John Hancock Tower is a wonderful vantage point to shoot from ... and apparently can produce winning shots :-).  Again, thanks all.', '2004-01-22 14:22:59.000000', '2004-01-22 14:25:41.000000', 0),
(553663, 54980, 6202, '<td valign="top">Critique Club<br/> <br/>Good detail and exposure here.  The snow grains are actually visable.  The shadows here add a good bit of interest to the photo. I think what would have made this even stronger would have been getting rid of the bottom left and moving in closer to the corn stalks and get a longer shadow.<br/> <br/>Nice work.</td>', 'Critique Club Good detail and exposure here.  The snow grains are actually visable.  The shadows here add a good bit of interest to the photo. I think what would have made this even stronger would have been getting rid of the bottom left and moving in closer to the corn stalks and get a longer shadow. Nice work.', '2004-01-22 14:52:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(553667, 55122, 6202, '<td valign="top">Critique Club.<br/> <br/>For starters this is overexposed to the point it is very harsh.  As for meeting the challenge it does and does so with interest.  Nice take on the challenge and an interesting composition.  Just watchout for the overexposure.</td>', 'Critique Club. For starters this is overexposed to the point it is very harsh.  As for meeting the challenge it does and does so with interest.  Nice take on the challenge and an interesting composition.  Just watchout for the overexposure.', '2004-01-22 14:54:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(553694, 54341, 10546, '<td valign="top">Hello again,<br/> <br/>I know about the focus lag. My experience with a Canon EOD300D tells me two things:<br/> <br/>A) It focus faster<br/>B) It wouldn''t save you on this one (ooppps... I''m going to get rammed here)<br/> <br/>I  would use single-auto-focus on the hand and then press the focus button and change to manual to lock it there. Works for me.</td>', 'Hello again, I know about the focus lag. My experience with a Canon EOD300D tells me two things: A) It focus fasterB) It wouldn''t save you on this one (ooppps... I''m going to get rammed here) I  would use single-auto-focus on the hand and then press the focus button and change to manual to lock it there. Works for me.', '2004-01-22 15:33:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(553801, 54976, 6624, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club:<br/> <br/>My first impression from this image is one of busy-ness...there is too much and no place for my eye to settle.  As the challenge was POV this means to me that you did not meet the challenge. That being said I think that you were after something here that doesn''t quite get across and I would like to know more of what you had in mind.  The title doesn''t help me either since I cannot apply the two meanings that I know for the phrase to your image. I don''t really want to suggest crops or other changes as I am so unsure of what you intended.<br/> <br/>Adding to the above:  I asked Anna more about her intent and discovered that the title refers to the cosmetic work done to RR cars.  This makes it a very clever title but one which is not supported by the image.  Her intent in point of view was to take it from an angle usually neglected and I feel that she accomplished this.  If the image could have been pared down, perhaps with a foreground crop to the iron fence, perhaps simplified and made to emphasize the rehab nature of the work then it might have been stronger.  The clarity and starkness are all there in contrast to the usual glamour of a "makeover" place. It was an interesting image to critique and I thank Anna for responding to my effort.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2004-01-22 19:06:16.</i></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club: My first impression from this image is one of busy-ness...there is too much and no place for my eye to settle.  As the challenge was POV this means to me that you did not meet the challenge. That being said I think that you were after something here that doesn''t quite get across and I would like to know more of what you had in mind.  The title doesn''t help me either since I cannot apply the two meanings that I know for the phrase to your image. I don''t really want to suggest crops or other changes as I am so unsure of what you intended. Adding to the above:  I asked Anna more about her intent and discovered that the title refers to the cosmetic work done to RR cars.  This makes it a very clever title but one which is not supported by the image.  Her intent in point of view was to take it from an angle usually neglected and I feel that she accomplished this.  If the image could have been pared down, perhaps with a foreground crop to the iron fence, perhaps simplified and made to emphasize the rehab nature of the work then it might have been stronger.  The clarity and starkness are all there in contrast to the usual glamour of a "makeover" place. It was an interesting image to critique and I thank Anna for responding to my effort.', '2004-01-22 17:22:04.000000', '2004-01-22 19:06:16.000000', 0),
(553815, 54128, 5158, '<td valign="top">Wonderfull perspective! When I first saw it, it took me quiet some time to relize how it was made and what it shows.<br/> <br/>BTW: I acctually been to Gruissan the last two summers: love that place!</td>', 'Wonderfull perspective! When I first saw it, it took me quiet some time to relize how it was made and what it shows. BTW: I acctually been to Gruissan the last two summers: love that place!', '2004-01-22 17:34:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(554179, 55074, 12268, '<td valign="top">thanks everyone for your comments!</td>', 'thanks everyone for your comments!', '2004-01-23 00:39:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(554322, 54460, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greeting from the Critique Club JC<br/>By Inspzil<br/> <br/>This incidentally is the 3rd time I''ve tried to do this picture.  Luck is not on my side<br/> <br/>Composition - This image to me needs to go one way or the other.  By that I mean that you have a nice abstract type pattern here, or you might have a good picture of the whole thing.  This seems to me to be a little of both and frankly I don''t think it works well.  You have some great color and great pattern to build on, but not much else.  The trees and stuff at the bottom need to be in there or they don''t.  From this distance, it''s difficult to determine what the subject actually is.  If it were closer, I''d say the metalwork.  Sometimes stuff at the bottom or in the corners of photos is there for perspective, but I can''t see what needs to be put in perspective on this shot.<br/> <br/>Technical - This is just a little bit soft, but nothing to write home about.  The framing looks a little crooked, probably by design.  I don''t think it really adds or detracts from the image at all.  The color is super.<br/> <br/>Overall - I don''t see where exactly you were going with this, which I think was a lot of the problem with your score.  I don''t like saying its subjectless, but I don''t have a better way to say it.  Getting right up to the steel girders would make a better argument that the girders were the subject.  I guess in the grand scheme of things, having a subject isn''t required for every picture.  On the other hand, the majority of non-landscape shots should have a definite subject or 2 even if just for something to focus on.  Take care and good luck - Bob</td>', 'Greeting from the Critique Club JCBy Inspzil This incidentally is the 3rd time I''ve tried to do this picture.  Luck is not on my side Composition - This image to me needs to go one way or the other.  By that I mean that you have a nice abstract type pattern here, or you might have a good picture of the whole thing.  This seems to me to be a little of both and frankly I don''t think it works well.  You have some great color and great pattern to build on, but not much else.  The trees and stuff at the bottom need to be in there or they don''t.  From this distance, it''s difficult to determine what the subject actually is.  If it were closer, I''d say the metalwork.  Sometimes stuff at the bottom or in the corners of photos is there for perspective, but I can''t see what needs to be put in perspective on this shot. Technical - This is just a little bit soft, but nothing to write home about.  The framing looks a little crooked, probably by design.  I don''t think it really adds or detracts from the image at all.  The color is super. Overall - I don''t see where exactly you were going with this, which I think was a lot of the problem with your score.  I don''t like saying its subjectless, but I don''t have a better way to say it.  Getting right up to the steel girders would make a better argument that the girders were the subject.  I guess in the grand scheme of things, having a subject isn''t required for every picture.  On the other hand, the majority of non-landscape shots should have a definite subject or 2 even if just for something to focus on.  Take care and good luck - Bob', '2004-01-23 08:20:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(554333, 55148, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>By Inspzil<br/> <br/>Composition - This is a really good shot of this dog, in terms of the composition.  You''ve captured the essence of the tired dog.  My dog does the same thing.  I think the crop is just a little tight on this one.  Also, the background in the top left corner is a bit too close tonally to the dogs fur.  That''s not that big of a deal, but it would help some.  The eyes are a little dead without any kinds of catchlights.  I don''t think this is a real big problem, but some folks would rake you over the coals for that one (i.e. my local camera club).  Even if they aren''t huge problems, it doesn''t hurt to try to make you at least aware of them.  Every little bit helps.<br/> <br/>Technical - This is where this image didn''t fare so well.  The focus is too soft.  I don''t know where your aperture was when you shot this.  It doesn''t appear the the focal plane was anywhere in the frame nor that your DOF was too shallow.  Its all pretty soft.  I''m curious to know if you took this in macro mode or not.  Looking at this, it leads me to believe that you didn''t and probably should have.  If you did... then blame the autofocus like any other photographer would &lt;laughs&gt;.  The exposure might have been just a little too long too.  That might be some of the reason for the blur.  Making this picture sharp is critical to its success.  The G2 is an extremely capable camera.  It might take just a little more patience (by yourself and the dog) to get this shot spot on perfect.<br/> <br/>Overall - You were really on the right track with this one.  You had the good scene, it just didn''t work out very well when you took the photo.  I think this photo could''ve gotten 5.8-6.1 if the photo quality was clean and crisp.  Its a very cute pic that just wasn''t executed as well as it could''ve been.  I think its worth trying to redo.  Take care and good luck - Bob</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubBy Inspzil Composition - This is a really good shot of this dog, in terms of the composition.  You''ve captured the essence of the tired dog.  My dog does the same thing.  I think the crop is just a little tight on this one.  Also, the background in the top left corner is a bit too close tonally to the dogs fur.  That''s not that big of a deal, but it would help some.  The eyes are a little dead without any kinds of catchlights.  I don''t think this is a real big problem, but some folks would rake you over the coals for that one (i.e. my local camera club).  Even if they aren''t huge problems, it doesn''t hurt to try to make you at least aware of them.  Every little bit helps. Technical - This is where this image didn''t fare so well.  The focus is too soft.  I don''t know where your aperture was when you shot this.  It doesn''t appear the the focal plane was anywhere in the frame nor that your DOF was too shallow.  Its all pretty soft.  I''m curious to know if you took this in macro mode or not.  Looking at this, it leads me to believe that you didn''t and probably should have.  If you did... then blame the autofocus like any other photographer would <laughs>.  The exposure might have been just a little too long too.  That might be some of the reason for the blur.  Making this picture sharp is critical to its success.  The G2 is an extremely capable camera.  It might take just a little more patience (by yourself and the dog) to get this shot spot on perfect. Overall - You were really on the right track with this one.  You had the good scene, it just didn''t work out very well when you took the photo.  I think this photo could''ve gotten 5.8-6.1 if the photo quality was clean and crisp.  Its a very cute pic that just wasn''t executed as well as it could''ve been.  I think its worth trying to redo.  Take care and good luck - Bob', '2004-01-23 08:39:16.000000', NULL, 0),
